In article <214@scorn.sco.COM> davidbe@sco.COM (The Cat in the Hat) writes:

>Ok...here's a "simple" way to have whetstones work.

>1)  They can enchant any item up to the magic level of the stone.  Thus
>    a +0 whetstone can resharpen a corroded weapon back to +0.  A +3 whetstone
>    is good to +3.  All whetstones enchant one class at a time.  To get
>    to +3 (as above) would require 3 seperate uses.

>5)  The + of the whetstone decreases by one each time it's used; a whetstone
>    has no charges.  This is necessary to help preserve game balance.

Say, you want to sharpen a +0 long sword with a +3 whetstone.
You apply the stone once:
	You have a +1 long sword and a +2 whetstone.
You apply the stone a second time:
	You have a +2 long sword and a +1 whetstone.

What happens next? Does a +1 whetstone damage the +2 long sword?
Doesn't seem to work..

What if a blessed whetstone would allow you to sharpen to +2, uncursed whetstone to +1 and
a cursed whetstone to +0. A blessed whetstone would *never* harm your weapon, but a cursed whetstone
would probably damage it (even to under +0). The 'plusses' of the whetstone would decrease with every 
usage.
